All of these tests were developed based on retrodictive relationships between theoretical tests and forecasting accuracy scores within an existing sample, as if those tests had been the ones administered to that sample. Next, we will evaluate the performance of these new tests on a new sample, comparing between versions and with the original version of the FPT. To do this, we will collect data on test performance as well as on forecasting accuracy from participants in a new study with four arms.
Each arm will contain three components: the (1) cognitive test of interest, (2) six forecasting items, and (3) cognitive tests not included in the tests of interest to serve as anchor items across participants.

The cognitive test of interest will either be the 15-minute test developed from the search algorithm, ridge regression, or XGBoost, or it will be the (which regular FPT version? the one with six forecasting items?).
Six quantile forecasting items.
The anchor items will be the Berlin Numeracy scale and the Cognitive Reflection Test.

We will collect data from participants on Prolific, and participants will be randomized into one of the four arms.






Some Open Questions About the Study Design





We know we want to have separate groups of participants take each new shortened FPT version, make some forecasts, and take some brief anchor cognitive tasks for comparison across groups, which is expected to take 50 minutes of testing time. What I’d love some input on is how to setup our “control” FPT conditions.
If we want to have a condition where participants take the most cognitive-task-heavy version of the FPT, that would start with 48 minutes of expected testing time. Then, six forecasting questions with the FPT paper’s estimated four minutes per question, would bring it to 72 minutes, and the anchor questions would total about 80 minutes of testing. Since this is quite a bit more than the shortened-FPT versions’ expected testing time, we would either be paying these participants more (probably not good incentive-wise), or we could have some filler task for the shortened-FPT participants, which would increase the total study cost.
Alternatively, or in addition to, we could use the shortest cognitive task FPT as a control, which is expected to take 12 minutes of cognitive testing, and overall would have about the same expected total time as the new short-FPT versions. If we only use this shortest-original-FPT as the control, we won’t have a direct comparison with how the existing FPTs would have performed at capturing forecasting accuracy on the items / at that time as the rest of the shortened versions, which seems not totally ideal, but also maybe isn’t necessarily super important?






Timeline for potential arms of the study. Below, timeline for the original proposed FPT versions, top to bottom as least to most cognitive testing.

